      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the technical side, shall we?\n\nIf it's not the solenoid or filter, another possible cause of the pressure spikes could be a faulty or worn-out valve body. The valve body is responsible for controlling the flow of fluid to the transmission's gears, and if it's not functioning correctly, it can cause uneven pressure distribution.\n\nIt's also possible that the issue is related to the transmission's clutch pack or bands. If they're worn out or not sealing properly, it can cause pressure fluctuations in the hydraulic circuit. You might want to check the clutch pack and bands for any signs of wear or damage.\n\nAnother possibility is a faulty or clogged master cylinder. The master cylinder is responsible for generating the pressure that drives the fluid through the transmission's hydraulic circuit. If it's not functioning correctly, it can cause pressure fluctuations.\n\nIn any case, I recommend using a scan tool to monitor the transmission's pressure sensors and look for any other error codes that might be related to the issue. This will give you a better understanding of what's going on and help you pinpoint the exact cause of the problem.\n\nBy the way, have you considered the possibility that the issue",
